# NIST SP 800-53 is the floor (800-171 / 800-172 are subsets)

## The rule

> **In all security-relevant design and implementation in this workspace,
> operate fully within NIST SP 800-53 Rev 5 (moderate baseline or
> stronger). CMMC, NIST SP 800-171, and NIST SP 800-172 control sets are
> subsets of 800-53. Satisfying 800-53 (or better) covers them. Never
> design a “171-only” or “CMMC-only” shortcut that weakens 800-53
> posture.**

Concretely:

1. **Floor = 800-53 Rev 5 moderate (or stronger).** When choosing
   auth, audit, crypto, access control, configuration, or logging
   behavior, map to the relevant 800-53 family (AC, AU, CM, IA, SC,
   SI, MP, …) first. Prefer the moderate baseline; raise to high /
   FedRAMP High where the deployment requires it.
2. **171 / 172 / CMMC are coverage checks, not alternate designs.**
   Use them to verify a 800-53-aligned design still hits CMMC Level 2
   (or 800-172 enhanced) practices. Do **not** implement a thinner
   control just because “CMMC only asks for X.”
3. **No weakening shortcuts.** Examples of forbidden reasoning:
   - “800-171 does not require session authenticity → skip SC-23”
   - “CMMC Level 2 omits this AC-16 marking → leave classification
     unenforced in a CMMC deployment”
   - “Commercial SKU can drop AU-9 append-only audit”
4. **Cross-link the enterprise contract.** US English, Section 508,
   FIPS 140-3, and AU-2/3/9/12 audit logging remain mandatory per
   [`enterprise-compliance.mdc`](enterprise-compliance.mdc). This rule
   sharpens the **800-53 floor** already stated there.

## Agent guidance

When adding or changing security-relevant code:

1. Name the **800-53 control(s)** in a short comment or doc note
   (e.g. `// NIST AC-16: security attributes on classified objects`).
2. If a ticket mentions only CMMC / 171 / 172, still implement to the
   **800-53** control that subsumes that practice.
3. Classification / labeling work uses AC-16 (and related MP-\*
   media protections for future DLP file markings). See
   `pim-offline-server/docs/dev/classification-and-800-53.md`.

## Forbidden constructs

- Designing auth, audit, or crypto to a **171-only** checklist while
  omitting the parent 800-53 control the checklist maps to.
- Documenting “CMMC compliant” as a substitute for “800-53 moderate
  (or better) aligned.”
- Removing or gating 800-53-required behavior behind a commercial /
  CMMC feature flag in a way that leaves a CMMC deployment weaker
  than 800-53 moderate.

## Worked example

**Wrong:** “CMMC AC.L2-3.1.1 is account management — implement
principals CRUD only; skip lockout because 171 wording is thinner.”

**Right:** Implement AC-2 (account management) **and** AC-7
(unsuccessful logon lockout) per 800-53 moderate; then confirm the
CMMC / 171 crosswalk still passes.

